# Solutions and Concentration: The Chemistry of Mixtures

> Almost everything we drink, inject, and clean with is a solution —
> one substance spread evenly through another. This lesson covers how
> solutions form, the many ways chemists express "how much is
> dissolved", and why dissolving things changes boiling and freezing
> points.

## 1. What Is a Solution?

[definition]
A **solution** is a homogeneous mixture of two or more substances —
uniform throughout, with particles of size < 1 nm that never settle.
The **solute** is the smaller (dissolved) component; the **solvent**
is the larger one doing the dissolving.
[/definition]

| Solute | Solvent | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Solid | Liquid | Sugar in water, salt in sea water |
| Gas | Liquid | Oxygen in water, CO₂ in soft drinks |
| Liquid | Liquid | Ethanol in water (whisky) |
| Solid | Solid | Alloys like brass (zinc in copper) |
| Gas | Gas | Air (mainly N₂ and O₂) |

Not to be confused with: **suspensions** (cloudy, particles settle —
muddy water, milk of magnesia) and **colloids** (particles ~1–1000 nm,
scatter light — milk, fog, mayonnaise, gelatin).

## 2. How Dissolving Works: "Like Dissolves Like"

Water molecules are polar. When salt dissolves, water's partial
charges surround each Na⁺ and Cl⁻, prising ions from the crystal
lattice — **hydration**. Non-polar solutes (grease, wax) can't be
hydrated, which is why oil and water refuse to mix, while grease
dissolves happily in non-polar solvents like petrol or hexane.

[info title="Why soap works"]
A soap molecule has a polar head and a long non-polar tail. The tails
embed in grease while heads stay in water, so grease breaks into
micelles that water can carry away — the chemistry behind every
dishwash.
[/info]

Solubility rises with temperature for most solids (more sugar
dissolves in hot tea), but falls for gases (warm soda goes flat, warm
water holds less oxygen for fish).

## 3. Expressing Concentration

"How much solute per how much solution" has several standard
vocabularies — each suited to different work:

### Mass percent

@math label="eq:mass-percent"
\text{mass \%} = \frac{\text{mass of solute}}{\text{mass of solution}} \times 100
@/math

5 g of salt in 95 g of water → 5% by mass. Common in labs and on
food labels.

### Parts per million (ppm)

For tiny concentrations — pollutants, minerals, chlorine in a pool:

@math label="eq:ppm"
\text{ppm} = \frac{\text{mass of solute}}{\text{mass of solution}} \times 10^6
@/math

Fluoride in drinking water is dosed at ~1 ppm; arsenic's safe limit is
10 ppb (parts per billion).

### Molarity (M) — the chemist's favourite

@math label="eq:molarity"
M = \frac{n_{\text{solute}}}{V_{\text{solution (L)}}}
@/math

A 1 M NaCl solution holds 1 mole (58.5 g) of NaCl per litre of
*solution*. Molarity links directly to reaction stoichiometry, since
equations count moles.

### Molality (m) — for temperature work

@math label="eq:molality"
m = \frac{n_{\text{solute}}}{\text{mass of solvent (kg)}}
@/math

Molality uses mass, not volume, so it never changes with temperature —
the right unit for freezing-point and boiling-point experiments.

[tip]
Dilution shortcut: $M_1V_1 = M_2V_2$. To turn concentrated 12 M HCl
into 1 L of 2 M acid, measure $V_1 = (2 \times 1)/12 = 0.167$ L and
dilute to the mark — always add acid to water, never water to acid.
[/tip]

## 4. Saturated, Unsaturated, Supersaturated

- **Unsaturated** — could still dissolve more solute at this
  temperature.
- **Saturated** — holds the maximum; extra solute sits on the bottom
  in equilibrium with dissolved particles.
- **Supersaturated** — a carefully cooled solution holding *more*
  than the normal maximum, metastable. Drop in a seed crystal and the
  excess crashes out — how rock candy and honey crystallisation work.

@chart line title="Grams of KNO₃ and NaCl dissolving in 100 g water vs temperature"
labels:
0
20
40
60
80

series:
  - name: "KNO₃"
    data: 13 32 64 110 169
  - name: "NaCl"
    data: 35 36 37 38 39
@/chart

The steep curve of potassium nitrate vs the nearly flat one of salt is
why **fractional crystallisation** separates them: cool a hot
saturated mixture and KNO₃ crystallises out while salt stays behind.

## 5. Colligative Properties: Numbers, Not Identity

Dissolved particles impede the solvent's escape and freezing — the
effects depend only on *how many* particles, not what they are:

- **Boiling point elevation:** a solution boils above the pure
  solvent's boiling point (adding salt raises water's bp slightly).
- **Freezing point depression:** a solution freezes below the pure
  solvent's freezing point — why salt melts road ice and why
  radiator antifreeze (ethylene glycol) protects engines both winter
  and summer.

[important]
Electrolytes multiply the effect. One mole of sugar gives 1 mole of
particles; one mole of NaCl gives ~2 moles of ions (Na⁺, Cl⁻), and
CaCl₂ gives ~3 — so salts depress freezing points several times
harder molecule-for-molecule than sugar.
[/important]

## 6. Solutions in the Body and Around Us

- **Blood plasma** is an aqueous solution; its ~0.9% NaCl
  concentration defines "normal saline" — matching it prevents cells
  from swelling or shrivelling.
- **Intravenous drips** are formulated in milliosmoles to be
  isotonic with blood.
- **Sea water** is ~3.5% dissolved salts; desalination by reverse
  osmosis pushes it through membranes that block ions.
- **Humidity** is water vapour dissolved in air; saturation gives
  100% relative humidity, and cooling supersaturates it into dew, fog,
  or rain.

[summary]
A solution is a homogeneous mixture of solute in solvent, governed by
"like dissolves like". Concentration is expressed as mass percent,
ppm, molarity (moles per litre of solution), or molality (moles per
kg of solvent), with $M_1V_1 = M_2V_2$ handling dilutions. Saturated
solutions sit at the solubility limit, supersaturated ones beyond it.
Dissolved particles raise boiling points and depress freezing points
in proportion to their count — colligative properties behind road
salt, antifreeze, and IV drips.
[/summary]
